  "tutorials": [
    {
      "dom_id": "step-12306",
      "name": "Introduction",
      "problem": "The exponential function  $f(x) = e^x$ has the special property that its derivative is the function itself:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( e^x \\right)= e^x\n$$\n\nSo, taking the derivative of an exponential function does nothing to the exponential function. The exponential function remains exactly the same. \n\nNote that the exponential function is the *only* function with this special property.\n\nWe'll justify this result at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice working with it.",
      "worked_solution": "",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": null,
      "proof_feedback": null,
