      "assets": [],
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214229/observations/lesson-step-12309-3eaa0bf1b49a656b415f.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214229/observations/lesson-step-12309-3eaa0bf1b49a656b415f.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/1114",
      "captured_at": 1792005876.8520222,
      "math_academy_id": 2612,
      "title": "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Base Other Than e",
      "content": "If we replace  the base $e$ of an exponential function by any other positive number $a$, then the derivative is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( a^x \\right)= a^x \\ln a\n$$\n\nWe just multiply the given exponential function by the natural logarithm of the base.\n\nNote that since $\\ln e =1$, our first formula is a particular case of this one!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( e^x \\right)= e^x \\ln e = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x\n$$"
    },
    {
      "dom_id": "step-12311",
      "name": "Justifying the Rule for the Derivative of the Exponential Function",
      "problem": "To derive the identity\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x,\n$$\n\nwe begin with the definition of the derivative:\n\nwhere $f(x) = e^x$.\n\nSubstituting the function $f(x)$ into the definition of $f'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x+h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot ({e}^{h}-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{h}-1}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{e^h - 1}{h} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x.\n$$",
