      ],
      "extraction_error_parts": {},
      "assets": [],
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214229/observations/lesson-step-12311-2311737674ec924fc220.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214229/observations/lesson-step-12311-2311737674ec924fc220.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/1114",
      "captured_at": 1792005878.214132,
      "math_academy_id": 2613,
      "title": "Justifying the Rule for the Derivative of the Exponential Function",
      "content": "To derive the identity\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x,\n$$\n\nwe begin with the definition of the derivative:\n\nwhere $f(x) = e^x$.\n\nSubstituting the function $f(x)$ into the definition of $f'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x+h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot ({e}^{h}-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{h}-1}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{e^h - 1}{h} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x.\n$$"
    },
    {
      "dom_id": "step-461037",
      "name": "Justifying the Second Rule",
      "problem": "Now, let's derive the rule\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214229/assets/77334bed6aafc3383552d90bcfc8da614d56493edce4e7ab89a2f28e01d3dbfa.svg)\n\nThe trick here is to write this in terms of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214229/assets/5ba79af546498192229d0cdd5a4a922d1d015802402b8eec5910fe00f999820d.svg) \n\nFirst, note that since the natural logarithm is the inverse of the exponential function, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214229/assets/091c69568c8472aacf8229a756a835e0edc26700677e4fa67de3d2f6ac0d96a2.svg)\n\nand by the power rule for logarithms, we can write this as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214229/assets/c9b146d550ef068823a4ee4b9f63eea89f46d6ceecedf0ad4baa6462f11d9127.svg)\n\nNow, the following result can be used (we'll prove it in the subsequent topics):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214229/assets/cedfc7ab127ac8b34cd72b21e8c3e527541e054721324d9d948d6df123cd61e0.svg)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214229/assets/5880fb665abda7f3cef4ad7425c99c43b16e84c448c7b9e22ff32f76e36df87c.svg)\n\nas required.",
      "worked_solution": "",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
